.Dd October 14, 2026
.Dt BOOTD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bootd
.Nd boot server for old and unusual machines
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Cm run
.Op Fl c Ar config
.Nm
.Cm check
.Op Fl c Ar config
.Op Fl q
.Nm
.Cm explain
.Op Fl c Ar config
.Ar client
.Op Ar service Op Ar file
.Nm
.Cm version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
network-boots old and unusual machines over many boot protocols, all set up
from one configuration file,
.Xr bootd.hcl 5 .
Each client is described once, with its addresses, the protocols it may use
and the files it may load, and every protocol answers from that one
description.
Every request that is refused is logged with the reason.
.Pp
The protocols are:
.Bl -tag -width bootparam -offset indent
.It Cm rarp
Reverse ARP, for Sun, DEC, HP and other clients that learn their IP
address from their Ethernet address.
.It Cm rmp
HP Remote Maintenance Protocol, for the boot ROMs of the HP 9000/300 and
/400.
.It Cm nd
Sun Network Disk, for the Sun-2 boot PROM.
Read only.
.It Cm tftp
TFTP, read only, with the blksize, tsize and timeout options.
.It Cm bootparam
Sun bootparam, with a built-in portmapper that answers broadcast
.Dv CALLIT
requests.
.It Cm nfs
NFS version 2 and MOUNT version 1 over UDP, read only.
.It Cm nis
NIS (YP), read only, without map transfers.
.El
.Pp
.Nm
works on Ethernet frames and runs its own IP stack, with its own Ethernet
and IP address on each network.
It answers ARP itself and knows the Ethernet address of every configured
host, so it reaches clients that do not answer ARP before their operating
system runs.
It needs no privileges and does not use the host's addresses or ports.
.Pp
A network carries frames over UDP, one Ethernet frame per datagram (the
format of the HECnet bridge, which simh and QEMU speak), optionally wrapped
in static L2TPv3.
.Xr bootbridge 8
connects a real network interface to it; emulators can send to
.Nm
directly.
A network can also capture on an interface with libpcap, which needs
privileges.
.Pp
The commands are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm run
Run the boot server.
This is the default when no command is given.
It runs in the foreground and logs to standard error unless the
configuration says otherwise.
.It Cm check
Validate the configuration, reporting errors and warnings with file and
line, then list every host with its addresses (and where they came from),
services and files.
.It Cm explain Ar client Op Ar service Op Ar file
Show how a request from
.Ar client ,
a host name, Ethernet address or IP address, would be handled, following
it through the same lookups the protocols use: which host it matches, and
that host's addresses, services and files.
With
.Ar service ,
also whether the service is enabled and allowed for the host, and which
file it would serve; with
.Ar file ,
which file a request for that name would get.
.It Cm version
Print the version.
.El
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c Ar config
The configuration file.
The default is
.Pa /etc/bootd.hcl .
.It Fl q
With
.Cm check ,
only print problems.
.El
.Sh SIGNALS
.Bl -tag -width SIGTERM
.It Dv SIGHUP
Reload the configuration's hosts, classes and files, and the NIS map
sources.
Changes to networks, services and logging take effect on restart.
.It Dv SIGINT , Dv SIGTERM
Exit.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /usr/local/etc/bootd.hcl -compact
.It Pa /etc/bootd.hcl
The default configuration file.
.It Pa /etc/ethers
Ethernet addresses of hosts that do not give one, see
.Xr ethers 5 .
.It Pa /var/lib/bootd
The root for boot files in the Debian and Ubuntu packages.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/bootd.hcl
The configuration file of the macOS package and of Homebrew on Intel
Macs.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 on success and 2 for invalid usage.
It exits 1 if the configuration has errors or the server fails, and
.Cm explain
exits 1 if the request would be refused.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Check a configuration and list what it serves:
.Pp
.Dl $ bootd check -c bootd.hcl
.Pp
Show how a TFTP request for a SPARCstation's boot program would be
handled:
.Pp
.Dl $ bootd explain kali tftp C0A80105.SUN4C
.Pp
Connect the Ethernet interface en0 to a
.Nm
on the same machine, then run
.Nm :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# bootbridge -i en0 -listen 127.0.0.1:4711 -peer 127.0.0.1:4712
$ bootd -c bootd.hcl
.Ed
.Pp
with this network in the configuration:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
network "lab" {
  address = "192.168.1.250/24"
  udp     = "127.0.0.1:4712"
  peers   = ["127.0.0.1:4711"]
}
.Ed
.Pp
Attach simh or QEMU to the same
.Nm :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sim> attach xq udp:4713:127.0.0.1:4712
qemu-system-sparc ... -netdev dgram,id=n0,local.type=inet,\e
    local.host=127.0.0.1,local.port=4713,remote.type=inet,\e
    remote.host=127.0.0.1,remote.port=4712
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bootd.hcl 5 ,
.Xr ethers 5 ,
.Xr bootbridge 8
.Pp
.Lk https://github.com/rpajarola/gobootd
.Sh STANDARDS
.Rs
.%R RFC 903
.%T A Reverse Address Resolution Protocol
.Re
.Rs
.%R RFC 1350
.%T The TFTP Protocol (Revision 2)
.Re
.Rs
.%R RFC 1094
.%T NFS: Network File System Protocol Specification
.Re
.Rs
.%R RFC 3931
.%T Layer Two Tunneling Protocol - Version 3 (L2TPv3)
.Re
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
is a Go reimplementation of an earlier bootd.
It is not a drop-in replacement.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Rico Pajarola Aq Mt rp@servium.ch
.Sh BUGS
NFS, ND and TFTP are read only: writable exports and disks are not
written to.
NIS cannot transfer maps to slave servers, and does not make
.Pa netgroup.byuser
and
.Pa netgroup.byhost .
